  "raw_text": "Step 1: Let \\( c \\) be a root of the quadratic equation \\( x^2 + ax + b = 0 \\). Then, by definition, we have:\n\\[ c^2 + ac + b = 0. \\]\n\nStep 2: Since \\( c^2 - 2 \\) is also a root, it must satisfy the same quadratic equation:\n\\[ (c^2 - 2)^2 + a(c^2 - 2) + b = 0. \\]\nExpanding this, we get:\n\\[ c^4 - 4c^2 + 4 + ac^2 - 2a + b = 0. \\]\nRearranging terms, we obtain:\n\\[ c^4 + (a - 4)c^2 + (4 - 2a + b) = 0. \\]\n\nStep 3: We now have two equations:\n1. \\( c^2 + ac + b = 0 \\)\n2. \\( c^4 + (a - 4)c^2 + (4 - 2a + b) = 0 \\)\n\nStep 4: From the first equation, express \\( b \\) in terms of \\( c \\) and \\( a \\):\n\\[ b = -c^2 - ac. \\]\n\nStep 5: Substitute \\( b = -c^2 - ac \\) into the second equation:\n\\[ c^4 + (a - 4)c^2 + (4 - 2a - c^2 - ac) = 0. \\]\nSimplify the equation:\n\\[ c^4 + (a - 4)c^2 + 4 - 2a - c^2 - ac = 0, \\]\n\\[ c^4 + (a - 5)c^2 - ac + 4 - 2a = 0. \\]\n\nStep 6: For the above equation to hold for all roots \\( c \\), the coefficients of corresponding powers of \\( c \\) must be zero. Therefore, we set up the system of equations:\n1. The coefficient of \\( c^4 \\): \\( 1 = 0 \\) (which is always true and does not provide new information).\n2. The coefficient of \\( c^3 \\): \\( -a = 0 \\Rightarrow a = 0 \\).\n3. The coefficient of \\( c^2 \\): \\( a - 5 = 0 \\Rightarrow a = 5 \\).\n4. The constant term: \\( 4 - 2a = 0 \\Rightarrow a = 2 \\).\n\nStep 7: We see that there is no consistent value of \\( a \\) that satisfies all these conditions simultaneously. This implies that the only possible values for \\( a \\) are those that make the polynomial structure consistent, which leads us to check specific values.\n\nStep 8: If \\( a = 0 \\), then from \\( b = -c^2 \\), substituting back, we get:\n\\[ c^2 - 2 \\text{ is also a root, so } (c^2 - 2)^2 = c^4 - 4c^2 + 4 = 0. \\]\nThis simplifies to \\( c^2 = 2 \\), giving \\( c = \\pm \\sqrt{2} \\).\n\nStep 9: If \\( a = 5 \\), then from \\( b = -c^2 - 5c \\), substituting back, we get:\n\\[ (c^2 - 2)^2 + 5(c^2 - 2) + (-c^2 - 5c) = 0. \\]\nThis simplifies to \\( c^4 + c^2 - 10c - 6 = 0 \\), which does not generally hold for all \\( c \\).\n\nStep 10: If \\( a = 2 \\), then from \\( b = -c^2 - 2c \\), substituting back, we get:\n\\[ (c^2 - 2)^2 + 2(c^2 - 2) + (-c^2 - 2c) = 0. \\]\nThis simplifies to \\( c^4 - 2c^2 - 2c = 0 \\), which does not generally hold for all \\( c \\).\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{2}<|im_end|>",
